Agent offline notification authority
===================================

Scope
-----
The detector commits an offline transition before queue submission and includes
its exact disconnected_at timestamp. The worker resolves the current agent, site
and organization from the database. It requires an enabled, live agent, active
live site and organization, consistent tenant ownership, the queued site and
heartbeat, the exact offline transition, and an unset notification marker.

Queued names, ages and channel settings are retained in the task signature for
compatibility but do not authorize a send. Current channel settings and the
current agent name are used; the current site supplies organization scope.
Disabled or missing channels do not fall back to the queued destinations.

Delivery outcomes
-----------------
At least one successful SENT or DELIVERED result is required. Every other result
must be successful SENT, DELIVERED or intentionally SKIPPED. Empty, all-skipped,
failed, bounced and pending batches do not set offline_notified_at. Tracking rows
are committed even for unsuccessful batches. A conditional update repeats the
transition, tenant and lifecycle conditions and checks the unchanged channel
configuration before marking delivery. Commit errors never report success.
Provider/database/broker exception text is not included in task outcomes or logs.

Deployment
----------
Deploy the detector and worker from the same release. Tasks queued by an older
detector without the exact disconnect timestamp are refused; they are not
silently applied to whatever offline event is current. Drain the old offline
notification queue before changing releases, or review and reissue affected
alerts under current authority. Refusal does not create a durable replacement.

Remaining limits
----------------
This is not a durable outbox or exactly-once delivery protocol. A broker failure
after the offline transition commits can still lose that alert. The detector
does not automatically reselect all offline agents with failed sends. Concurrent
workers can both produce external effects before one wins the conditional
marker update. A send accepted by a provider followed by a database failure is
uncertain and may duplicate on a later attempt. A partial delivery can likewise
duplicate a successful recipient if the whole alert is retried.

Authority is checked before dispatch and again for the marker; edits after the
first check cannot retract an external message already in flight. The generic
in-app helper still controls its own commit and event behavior. This change does
not claim transaction atomicity across notifications, external providers and
business records, or solve concurrent heartbeat versus stale-detector updates.
Those paths need separate lifecycle and crash-recovery qualification.

Tests use owned synthetic recipients and real database fixtures where stated.
They do not send production mail, webhooks or SMS and do not establish provider
interoperability or a customer SLA.
